Jonadab the Unsightly One wrote:
>
>> Early wizards live by evasion.
>
> Okay, but there's something about this tactic that
> I don't understand.  Many early-game monsters are
> faster than you, and they consistently ignore your
> pet and come straight after you.  How do you evade?
> You can only run until they reach you, and there's
> typically nowhere to hide.  Closed doors only work
> until a monster is generated on your side of the
> door, or until an orc shows up, whichever comes
> first.

Stay against walls so fewer can get adjacent to you.  Use corridors as
much as possible.  Sometimes when retreating into corridors swap places
with your pet if needed to cast force bolt then swap places with your
pet as a shield.  Sometimes when retreating into corridors swap places
with your pet to use it as a shield.

You won't be able to get melee down to zero but the less the better.  As
a wizard you do want a long term plan of enhancing levels so you do want
experience.  Make as much of it as possible casting and throwing
something-anything.  Plus let your pet kill a lot of monsters to be able
to protect you in the mines.  I still have a very high mortality rate
when entering the mines with wizards so I'm still getting it wrong
myself.

> You can cast force bolt a few times, but soon
> you run out of both power and food, and now you
> are weak from hunger *and* have two hitpoints
> left.

You have a very different experience with hungerless casting than I do. 
I consider doing Sokoban before the mines for the extra food.  When
entering the mines I cast a lot, pick up a corpse and step back up the
stairs.  It takes a lot of those cycles before I level up and can handle
exploring the first level.  The experience of doing Sokoban first
matters more for low AC characters than it does for combat wombats.

> Unless you got really lucky with your starting wand

Use up every resource early.  In Nethack items are like Doritos - "Munch
a bunch.  We'll make more".  Every turn you survive makes surviving the
rest of the game more likely.

> your Elbereth prowess is limited to writing in the dust.

To be done often.  Primary way to give your pet experience is to write
it in the dust and stay put healing while your pet fights.  Yes, I'm
giving contradictory advice.  That's how Nethack works.


